ghpull:433 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "Add full-circle Manchester Syntax round-trip fidelity test" ;
    bl:number 433 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:179 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr433 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:179 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-09T07:59:45Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-09T08:09:29Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr433Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #179 asked for an end-to-end fidelity test of the whole Manchester pipeline: parse -> owl2rdf -> Turtle -> re-parse -> rdf2owl -> serialize back to Manchester, then compare against the original. Its own discussion flagged 'what does compare mean' as the open design question and suggested starting with structural axiom-set comparison rather than byte-for-byte text, canonicalising blank-node/anonymous-individual numbering the way rdf_canon does for graph isomorphism. rdf_canon's API turned out not to fit directly -- it canonicalises RDF quads/blank nodes, but Ontology's Vec<Axiom> has no quad-shaped view to hand it (routing the comparison itself back through RDF would just reintroduce the numbering churn being canonicalised away). Applied the same 'stable renumbering keyed by first appearance' pattern locally instead: a small recursive AnonRenumberer walking every axiom shape that can mention an Individual, comparing HashSet<Axiom> after renumbering. Two fixtures: a rich no-anonymous-individual ontology covering class/property/individual axioms owl2rdf's current scope actually translates, and a minimal single-anonymous-individual fixture isolating the renumbering path (excluding the individual's declaration axiom from comparison, since RDF has no declaration triple for a blank node -- not a bug, by design). Running the pipeline surfaced two real bugs, both fixed inline as small, scoped corrections: rdf_owl_translator silently dropped owl:differentFrom on ingestion (missing from both the structural-predicate allowlist and extract_axiom's match), so DifferentFrom: axioms vanished on the RDF->OWL leg; and class_expression_parser fell back to owl:Literal for any datatype IRI lacking an explicit `a rdfs:Datatype` declaration triple, which xsd:integer never gets since owl2rdf only declares entities the ontology itself introduces, not referenced XSD vocabulary -- fixed by recognising any IRI in the xsd: namespace as a NamedDataRange directly." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:433 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr433 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr433Decision .

session:pr433Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Compared HashSet<Axiom> of the original vs. round-tripped Ontology (order-independent structural equality), with anonymous-individual ids canonicalised by first-occurrence order via a small local recursive walk over the Axiom/ClassExpression/Assertion tree." ;
    agp:alternative "Reusing rdf_canon's quad-shaped canonicalisation directly, as the issue suggested trying first, was rejected: Ontology's axiom tree has no natural RDF-quad representation to hand it, and mapping through RDF for the comparison itself would reintroduce the very blank-node numbering churn the canonicalisation step exists to remove. A looser semantic-equivalence comparison (diffing owl2datalog-materialised consequences instead of raw axioms) was noted per the issue as a possible stronger follow-up, not attempted here." .
